WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:05.590
With everything from superhero team-ups to
record-breaking horror releases, 2017 has

00:00:05.590 --> 00:00:10.080
been a big year for movies—and an even bigger
year for all the Easter eggs they hid.

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:15.080
It's a lot to go through, so we rounded up
some of the all-time best movie Easter eggs

00:00:15.080 --> 00:00:16.140
of 2017.

00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:17.340
Some spoilers ahead.

00:00:18.380 --> 00:00:19.460
A mop and a beer

00:00:19.880 --> 00:00:24.699
The fifth entry in the Pirates of the Caribbean
franchise featured a heavily CGI'd version

00:00:24.699 --> 00:00:28.730
of a young Johnny Depp as Captain Jack Sparrow,
but that wasn't the only glimpse into the

00:00:28.730 --> 00:00:29.540
actor's past.

00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:35.440
Just before Sparrow is set to be executed,
his Uncle Jack--played by ex-Beatle Paul McCartney--tells

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:35.940
him a joke:

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:37.580
"Did I ever tell you the one about the skeleton?"

00:00:38.380 --> 00:00:39.640
"Yes you have many time…"

00:00:39.640 --> 00:00:42.920
"Skeleton goes into a bar, orders a beer and
a mop."

00:00:45.620 --> 00:00:49.860
It sounds like your average bad pirate joke,
but it's actually an Easter egg with roots

00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:53.260
20 years in the past that started with Al
Pacino, of all people.

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:58.240
While Depp and Pacino were filming 1997's
Donnie Brasco, Pacino would tell that joke

00:00:58.250 --> 00:01:00.270
repeatedly, then laugh and walk off.

00:01:00.270 --> 00:01:01.280
Depp later admitted:

00:01:01.340 --> 00:01:02.580
"I didn't get the joke!

00:01:02.590 --> 00:01:03.969
He thought it was my problem.

00:01:03.969 --> 00:01:07.060
He inflicted it upon me and I could feel my
IQ points drop…

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:09.360
He would howl every time he told the joke."

00:01:09.560 --> 00:01:13.400
What better way to return the favor than to
inflict it on millions of moviegoers?

00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:17.660
Even better, the guy laughing at the joke
in Pirates of the Caribbean sounds a lot like

00:01:17.660 --> 00:01:18.260
Pacino.

00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:21.980
"Funny as ever."

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:23.500
Play time

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:29.160
Hot on the heels of 2016's Deadpool, Logan
proved once again that it's okay for a superhero

00:01:29.170 --> 00:01:34.130
to spill buckets of blood and swear like a
sailor without sacrificing those ever-important

00:01:34.130 --> 00:01:34.860
ticket sales.

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:39.860
Hopefully, this will kick off a new trend
in superhero films, but Logan wasn't entirely

00:01:39.860 --> 00:01:43.880
focused on the future—it also had a few
pretty sweet callbacks to days of Wolverine

00:01:43.880 --> 00:01:44.380
past.

00:01:44.420 --> 00:01:48.940
While the comic books Laura picks up play
a big part in the plot, you may not have noticed

00:01:48.950 --> 00:01:53.360
another Easter egg that popped up: during
Logan's funeral, you can see one of the mutant

00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:57.020
children holding a Wolverine action figure—complete
with yellow spandex.

00:01:57.180 --> 00:02:01.790
Considering this is most likely Hugh Jackman's
last outing with the adamantium claws, it

00:02:01.790 --> 00:02:06.400
was a nice touch to put Wolverine's early
comics outfit onscreen, even if it only showed

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:08.320
up briefly, and on a plastic toy.

00:02:09.500 --> 00:02:10.540
Journey mountain

00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:16.080
Director Jordan Vogt-Roberts is a self-proclaimed
video game fanatic, and his sophomore feature

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:21.560
film Kong: Skull Island is jam-packed with
video game nods, with references to everything

00:02:21.560 --> 00:02:23.720
from Resident Evil to The Legend of Zelda.

00:02:23.800 --> 00:02:27.911
But the best Easter egg in the film doesn't
come from a massive franchise—rather, it's

00:02:27.911 --> 00:02:33.170
a subtle nod to Journey, a game in which players
have to cross a huge open world while communicating

00:02:33.170 --> 00:02:37.350
with other players via musical notes, all
while trying to reach a glowing mountain in

00:02:37.350 --> 00:02:38.080
the distance.

00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:42.360
Perhaps to emphasize the journey the characters
in Kong had to take to cross Skull Island,

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:46.510
Vogt-Roberts stuck a re-creation of Journey's
mountain in the distance in a few scenes of

00:02:46.510 --> 00:02:47.010
the movie.

00:02:47.140 --> 00:02:51.700
It's most visible when the helicopters first
breach the storm around the island, and again

00:02:51.700 --> 00:02:53.400
at the end when the characters are leaving.

00:02:53.500 --> 00:02:57.620
It's subtle, but awesome once you've spotted
it—a perfect example of what an Easter egg

00:02:57.620 --> 00:02:58.140
should be.

00:02:59.100 --> 00:03:00.140
Musical cameo

00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:05.220
It's the music of Baby Driver that brings
the film to life, which is particularly fitting

00:03:05.220 --> 00:03:08.580
considering the core premise has its origins
in a music video.

00:03:08.680 --> 00:03:14.660
In 2002, Edgar Wright—who helmed Baby Driver—directed
a music video for "Blue Song" by British band

00:03:14.660 --> 00:03:15.440
Mint Royale.

00:03:15.500 --> 00:03:20.720
The narrative of the video is this: a sunglasses-wearing
driver takes a group of thieves to a heist,

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:23.080
then waits in the car and lip-syncs to a song.

00:03:23.260 --> 00:03:24.100
Sound familiar?

00:03:24.280 --> 00:03:29.100
That sequence inspired the opening scene in
Baby Driver, which sees Baby rocking out to

00:03:29.100 --> 00:03:33.710
"Bellbottoms" by Jon Spencer Blues Explosion
while the rest of the crew pulls off the heist.

00:03:33.710 --> 00:03:37.060
Even better, Wright didn't pay homage to that
video in style only.

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:41.660
During a scene in Baby's apartment, the "Blue
Song" music video can briefly be seen playing

00:03:41.660 --> 00:03:42.460
on the TV.

00:03:43.720 --> 00:03:44.660
Spider-Signal

00:03:44.940 --> 00:03:48.580
You may have missed this quick Easter egg
in Spider-Man Homecoming, because it was only

00:03:48.580 --> 00:03:50.060
visible for about a second.

00:03:50.180 --> 00:03:54.280
In the scene when Spider-Man is locked in
the warehouse in DC, he starts tinkering with

00:03:54.280 --> 00:03:55.600
his suit to pass the time.

00:03:57.120 --> 00:03:57.620
"What?"

00:03:58.080 --> 00:03:59.780
"Maybe we should run that refresher course."

00:03:59.980 --> 00:04:04.120
That's the Spider-Signal, also known as the
worst superhero gadget ever.

00:04:04.310 --> 00:04:10.260
The Spider-Signal first appeared in The Amazing
Spider-Man #3, published in 1963, in which

00:04:10.260 --> 00:04:14.290
the signal is literally nothing more than
a warning to criminals that Spider-Man is

00:04:14.290 --> 00:04:15.280
somewhere nearby.

00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:19.260
Usually--surprise!--at the other end of the
beam of light projecting the signal.

00:04:19.380 --> 00:04:23.400
So why is this such a great Easter egg compared
to everything else in Homecoming, like the

00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:26.880
Ultron head Spidey pulls out of a bag later
in the same scene?

00:04:26.980 --> 00:04:31.260
Mainly because it's such a useless detail
to add, and it's all the more amazing because

00:04:31.260 --> 00:04:31.760
of that.

00:04:32.920 --> 00:04:33.820
Final farewell

00:04:34.320 --> 00:04:38.620
The Guardians of the Galaxy movies have a
ton of Easter eggs, but one of the best recurring

00:04:38.620 --> 00:04:41.380
eggs in the franchise showed up at Yondu's
funeral.

00:04:41.500 --> 00:04:45.660
One of Yondu's favorite trinkets in the first
film was that little blue crystal frog that

00:04:45.660 --> 00:04:47.000
he got at the beginning of the movie.

00:04:47.120 --> 00:04:49.020
You got any other cute little buggers like
this un?

00:04:49.700 --> 00:04:53.240
I like to stick em all in a row on my control
console."

00:04:53.600 --> 00:04:58.590
In Guardians 2, just before Yondu is blasted
into space, the camera lingers for a second

00:04:58.590 --> 00:05:02.160
on that same frog, sitting by his side just
as it did in life.

00:05:02.350 --> 00:05:06.000
Among the hundreds of Easter eggs in Marvel
movies, this is the only one that'll bring

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:07.120
a tear to your eye.

00:05:08.340 --> 00:05:09.260
Walt's initials

00:05:09.800 --> 00:05:14.280
Spotting secrets in any Disney film is always
fun, and the studio rarely disappoints.

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:19.220
In 2017's live-action Beauty and the Beast,
one of the biggest Easter eggs in the film

00:05:19.220 --> 00:05:21.480
is built right into the woodwork, so to speak.

00:05:21.640 --> 00:05:26.420
In the Beast's massive, ornate ballroom, pay
attention to the monogram inscribed on the

00:05:26.420 --> 00:05:27.120
ballroom floor.

00:05:27.160 --> 00:05:31.200
Hidden within the flowery design is a big
"WD"—short for Walt Disney.

00:05:31.400 --> 00:05:34.960
Honestly, we'd chalk this one up to a trick
of the eye if this Easter egg hadn't been

00:05:34.960 --> 00:05:39.000
confirmed by none other than Sarah Greenwood,
production designer for the film.

00:05:40.100 --> 00:05:40.600
Bookwhacked

00:05:41.540 --> 00:05:46.220
By every reasonable measure, the Tom Cruise-led
reboot of The Mummy was a complete failure.

00:05:46.480 --> 00:05:50.700
Critics bashed the film for everything from
the muddled story to its shameless ripoffs

00:05:50.700 --> 00:05:54.560
of previous plots, but there was one little
highlight in the movie that might warrant

00:05:54.560 --> 00:05:55.260
a re-watch.

00:05:55.600 --> 00:05:56.220
Maybe.

00:05:56.560 --> 00:05:57.260
If you hate yourself.

00:05:57.480 --> 00:06:01.780
In the scene when Dr. Jekyll goes full-on
Hyde, Annabelle Wallis' character, Jenny,

00:06:01.780 --> 00:06:04.520
knocks a guy out by whacking him on the head
with a big book.

00:06:04.640 --> 00:06:08.960
Look closely, because that book has made an
onscreen appearance in the past—it's the

00:06:08.960 --> 00:06:12.120
Book of the Dead, first seen in 1999's The
Mummy.

00:06:12.280 --> 00:06:14.200
You know, the one with Brendan Fraser.

00:06:14.320 --> 00:06:16.260
Who would have thought we'd ever miss that
version?

00:06:16.700 --> 00:06:19.280
"Ah.."

00:06:19.680 --> 00:06:21.000
"Ah.."

00:06:24.180 --> 00:06:25.320
Gunshot sonata

00:06:25.640 --> 00:06:29.260
John Wick: Chapter 2 should be a case study
for sequels done right.

00:06:29.400 --> 00:06:32.880
It took everything great about the first film
and made it even better.

00:06:32.980 --> 00:06:37.560
A lot of that comes down to the incredible
detail put into building the franchise's hyper-kinetic

00:06:37.560 --> 00:06:40.720
world—details that most fans likely won't
even notice.

00:06:40.920 --> 00:06:45.220
Probably the best example came at the end
of John Wick 2, during the gun battle in the

00:06:45.220 --> 00:06:45.720
museum.

00:06:45.820 --> 00:06:50.600
The shootout is set to Vivaldi's Four Seasons,
only the percussion is replaced by the sound

00:06:50.600 --> 00:06:54.240
of Wick's gun, literally turning his shots
into the musical score.

00:07:01.720 --> 00:07:06.740
It's an insanely obscure Easter egg, but a
fantastic example of an off-kilter detail

00:07:06.740 --> 00:07:09.060
that only the filmmakers are likely to ever
notice.

00:07:09.200 --> 00:07:11.240
As director Chad Stahelski put it,

00:07:11.240 --> 00:07:15.640
"If somebody does pick it up, it'll be two
guys going like, holy s—, that's Vivaldi!"

00:07:16.740 --> 00:07:17.520
What's in a name?

00:07:18.100 --> 00:07:23.020
We know—Ready Player One isn't getting released
until 2018, but its Easter eggs are already

00:07:23.020 --> 00:07:27.920
spilling over into the film's marketing: when
the film's logo was released in July 2017,

00:07:27.960 --> 00:07:31.540
fans were quick to spot an Easter egg hiding
right in the name of the film.

00:07:31.660 --> 00:07:36.340
If you look closely, the letters in "Ready
Player One" form a maze that runs to a literal

00:07:36.340 --> 00:07:38.120
egg in the center of the "O" in "One."

00:07:38.340 --> 00:07:44.540
As noted by Gizmodo, that's a nod to Adventure,
a 1979 Atari game that sees the player moving

00:07:44.540 --> 00:07:46.400
through the maze-like rooms of a castle.

00:07:46.520 --> 00:07:51.120
More importantly, Adventure is widely believed
to contain the first Easter egg in gaming

00:07:51.120 --> 00:07:55.449
history, and in the Ready Player One book,
Adventure is a massive inspiration for one

00:07:55.449 --> 00:07:56.200
of the characters.

00:07:56.460 --> 00:08:00.080
We don't want to give too much else away,
so you'll have to read the book or wait until

00:08:00.080 --> 00:08:02.910
March 2018 to see how all those pieces connect.

00:08:03.220 --> 00:08:04.140
Thanks for watching!

00:08:04.320 --> 00:08:07.020
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:08:07.120 --> 00:08:09.420
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

